(a) The wrecker or tow truck company will provide proof of compliance with all Department of Public Safety (DPS) rules to include proper marking of towing vehicles; proof of Georgia Intrastate Motor Carrier Registration (GIMCR) or Unified Carrier Registration (UCR).
(b) The wrecker or tow truck company shall maintain adequate liability insurance on both the towing vehicles and each storage facility or impound lot as required by the Georgia Department of Public Safety and the Federal Motor Carrier Safety Administration (FMCSA). The following amounts will be considered minimums:
• $100,000.00 bodily injury to any one person;
• $300,000.00 bodily injury to two or more persons;
• $50,000.00 property damage; and
• $50,000.00 Garage Keepers Legal Liability (GKLL) insurance on each storage area, with coverage sufficient for the actual value of all stored vehicles towed or removed to such storage area, as well as the contents of such vehicles.
• For all vehicles used in the interstate business of the wrecker service, $1,000,000.00 liability insurance.
• Form MCS-90 (Proof of Financial Responsibility for motor carriers) must be filed electronically with FMCSA.
(c) The wrecker or tow truck operator's storage facility, if inside the city, shall adhere to environmental ordinances already established, including but not limited to section 10-21-14.
(d) The wrecker or tow truck operator shall allow inspection during regular business hours of facilities and/or documents by the chief of police, or his designee, to ensure compliance with this agreement and/or applicable city ordinances.
(e) The wrecker or tow truck operator shall have response time to the scene of not more than thirty (30) minutes during normal business hours M-F 8:00 a.m. to 5:00 p.m. and no longer than thirty-five (35) minutes for all other days and times.
(f) The wrecker or tow truck operator shall have a tow vehicle available for response twenty-four (24) hours a day, seven (7) days per week. Should the operator be unable to respond for any reason or within the required timeframe, the wrecker company will notify 911 and request to be removed from the towing list for the period of time it is unable to respond. If unavailable for a timely response as required herein, a wrecker company may not pass the block towing call to another wrecker or tow truck operator.
(g) Each wrecker or tow truck shall be equipped with items needed to clean the roadway of debris resulting from traffic crashes or vehicle inoperability. The wrecker or tow truck operator will clean the roadway of all debris as required by Georgia law.
(h) The wrecker or tow truck operator shall dispose of abandoned vehicles in accordance with applicable Georgia law.
(i) The wrecker or tow truck operator shall abide by and submit to any lawful "police hold" placed on any impounded vehicle for investigation purposes and shall not release or grant access to the "held" vehicle until such "hold" has been released by the requesting law enforcement agency. Release from this "hold" will be documented via email from the officer who placed it to the towing service.
(j) The wrecker or tow truck operator shall make all requests for any additional assistance in recovering a vehicle to the officer in charge of the scene who will call for an additional wrecker through E-911. A wrecker service is prohibited from calling another service directly to request assistance. Should another wrecker operator wish to provide voluntary assistance to the primary wrecker operator in order to more efficiently facilitate the clearing of an accident scene, the vehicle owner(s) shall only be charged by the primary operator according to the rates in this block towing ordinance.
(k) Drivers for the wrecker or tow truck operator shall possess and maintain all necessary licenses and certifications required by law to perform the duties outlined in this block towing ordinance in addition to and including the following:
(1) Wreck Master Certification,
(2) Rollback Driver Basic 2/3 4/5 or TRIP 1
(3) Heavy Wrecker 6/7 or TRIP 2
(4) Traffic Incident Management Certification
(5) Flagger Certification
(6) Nonconsensual Towing (NCT) Certification
(l) Wreckers, tow truck, and equipment. The following items and equipment must be maintained on all wreckers and tow trucks:
(1) Each vehicle must be permanently marked on both sides of the body or cab of the vehicle, in letters and figures in sharp color contrast to the background and legible from a distance of fifty (50) feet during daylight hours while the vehicle is stationary. This lettering must show the name of the wrecker company, its address, telephone number, and the company's state or federal USDOT number;
(2) Valid registration and revalidation decal displayed on the rear of the vehicle and clearly visible;
(3) All light permits as required by law;
(4) Flashing or revolving amber lights with 360-degree visibility and in compliance with all other requirements of Georgia law, including but not limited to O.C.G.A. section 40-8-35;
(5) Battery jumper cables;
(6) Fire extinguisher;
(7) Sweeping broom;
(8) Flat shovel;
(9) Container for debris;
(10) Motorcycle tie-down straps (4);
(11) Tow away lamps (for vehicles being towed);
(12) Stopped vehicle warning devices (reflective triangles);
(13) Sufficient amount of "Oil Dri," "Stay-Dry," sand, or other absorbent medium for vehicle fuel or other spills.
(Ord. No. 25-07, § 2, 2-11-25)

